<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-making-sense-of-differences" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Making Sense of Variation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Variation is how observations differ from one another.
      If each treatment always gave the same yield, one plot per treatment would
      be enough to tell which yielded more.
      In practice, one plot may yield more than another because of the
      treatment, because of its growing conditions, or both.
      As Chapter 5 showed, replication helps researchers judge a difference
      between treatments against the variation among plots given the same
      treatment.
    </p>

    <p>
      We examine variation to judge what a comparison can tell us and to learn
      from differences that an average alone cannot describe.
      Accounting for relevant differences in growing conditions can help
      estimate a treatment effect more precisely.
      The analysis may also reveal that the treatment works differently under
      different conditions.
    </p>

    <p>
      When researchers brought Roger Stern their data for analysis, he would
      ask, <q>Can you show me the objectives?</q>
      Some were surprised.
      They had already done the study and wanted him to analyse the results.
      For Roger, the objectives were where that work began.
      Which questions could the data still answer?
      Had anything emerged that was worth investigating?
    </p>

    <p>
      Analysis is how researchers make sense of variation.
      The same observations can be grouped and summarised in several ways, each
      revealing something different about the data.
      Modelling establishes how much variation the recorded information accounts
      for.
      The study objectives guide how observations should be grouped, summarised
      and modelled.
      These choices help distinguish what can be attributed to a treatment or
      the setting and what remains unaccounted for.
    </p>

    <p>
      The variation that remains is also a source of information.
      When the remaining variation follows a pattern, the next question is
      whether that pattern matters to the original comparison.
      Taking it into account may make the result clearer or change the
      conclusion.
      Sometimes its main value is to show what could be investigated next.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-making-sense-of-differences-informative-grouping">
    <title>Finding an Informative Grouping</title>

    <p>
      <em>Source podcast: RMS and Multilevel Data</em>
    </p>

    <audio href="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/13748/291-understanding-multilevel-data-through-context.mp3" width="60%" />

    <p>
      While working with researchers from millet trials in Niger, David
      encountered yields that varied widely from plot to plot.
      The study involved thousands of farmers, but information about individual
      farms was limited.
      The records did, however, identify each farmer's organisation as the data
      had come through a farmer federation.
      They chose to group the data by farmer organisation.
      This revealed a pattern: yields were relatively similar within groups but
      differed markedly between them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Knowing the groups helped interpret the pattern.
      Some were women's groups whose members helped one another manage fields to
      feed their families.
      Others brought farmers together to pool millet for sale.
      The women's groups described in this account had limited access to good
      land and inputs, while some commercially oriented groups had better land
      and used fertiliser.
      Organisation membership connected the plot yields to these farming
      circumstances, even where detailed information about farms was limited.
    </p>

    <p>
      A yield that looked low across the full dataset could be typical among
      members of that farmer's organisation.
      Grouping made that distinction visible.
      Part of the overall spread lay between organisations, and part remained
      among farmers in the same organisation.
      In this sense, organisation membership helped account for variation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Accounting for variation is distinct from explaining what caused it.
      An organisation may bring together farmers who already have good land and
      access to inputs, or it may help members obtain those resources.
      Either could produce a pattern of higher yields among its members.
      The yield comparison alone cannot distinguish these possibilities or
      establish that joining the organisation would increase another farmer's
      yield.
    </p>

    <p>
      Membership was already part of how the federation organised its records.
      A researcher collecting plot measurements independently might have
      overlooked it.
      In trials across diverse farms, deciding which farming circumstances may
      help account for differences in yield can guide what to record and link to
      each plot.
    </p>

    <p>
      The yields could also have been grouped by village.
      Village could be informative where farmers share conditions relevant to
      the results, such as soil fertility or access to roads and inputs.
      However, in this case several organisations may operate within one
      village, and an organisation may span several villages.
      Therefore grouping by village would have made the pattern associated with
      farmer organisation harder to see.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same reasoning applies at a wider scale.
      An administrative region provides one way to group farms, but an
      agroecological zone may better represent the rainfall conditions under
      which crops are grown.
      If rainfall is important to interpreting yield variation, that
      classification may be more informative than an administrative boundary.
    </p>

    <p>
      Neither grouping is automatically better.
      The choice depends on what each represents.
      David encourages people to consider: <q>what is it that you actually want
      to capture?</q>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-making-sense-of-differences-zeros">
    <title>Zeros and the Mean of What?</title>

    <introduction>

      <p>
      <em>Source podcasts: RMS and the Problem of Zeros; Levels of Variability</em>
      </p>

      <audio href="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/14898/293-the-problem-of-zeros-in-data.mp3" width="60%" />

      <audio href="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/8019/182-twenty-years-of-rms-for-crfs-levels-of-variability.mp3" width="60%" />

      <p>
      Calculating a mean or a standard deviation can be straightforward.
      Deciding which observations to include requires thought, because that
      choice determines what the summary represents.
      <q>The mean of what?</q> is a simple but important question.
      It asks researchers to identify what they are averaging and which
      population the figure describes.
      </p>

      <p>
      In the Niger trials, the federation's records supplied the grouping.
      Sometimes the observations themselves suggest a grouping.
      Roger uses a small teaching example.
      Six farmers have 0, 10, 0, 15, 0 and 20 animals.
      The zeros identify the farmers who own no livestock.
      </p>

      <p>
      Together the six farmers own 45 animals.
      Dividing by six gives a mean of 7.5 animals per farmer.
      That figure is useful when the enquiry concerns livestock numbers across
      all farmers.
      Grouping the farmers by ownership describes two other features of the same
      data: half the farmers own livestock, and those who do have 15 animals on
      average.
      </p>

      <p>
      The same choice matters when examining spread.
      A standard deviation calculated across all six farmers combines the
      difference between having animals and having none with differences in herd
      size among owners.
      Reporting ownership separately makes the first feature visible.
      Among owners, herd sizes range from 10 to 20 animals.
      </p>

      <p>
      Grouping by ownership helps account for some of the variation and raises
      two more specific questions.
      Why do some farmers own livestock?
      What accounts for differences in herd size among those who do?
      Roger welcomes variability for this reason.
      <q>I like a lot of spread in my data because then I've got something to
      explain.</q>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-making-sense-of-differences-complementary-summaries">
      <title>Retaining Complementary Summaries</title>

      <p>
        In a field study Roger recalls, the researchers had measured total plot
        yield and wanted to calculate average yield per plant.
        Each plot started with 100 plants, but some died.
        If 25 plants died, should the researchers divide the yield by the
        original 100 or by the 75 survivors?
      </p>

      <p>
        Dividing by 100 gives production per plant originally planted, including
        the loss associated with the deaths.
        Dividing by 75 gives production per surviving plant.
        Two plots can have the same yield per surviving plant and quite
        different total yields if more plants died in one of them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Roger suggested keeping both calculations and recording how many plants
        died.
        As Chapter 4 argued, an additional measurement earns its place through
        what it allows a study to establish.
        Here, the death counts would show the proportion of plants that survived
        and help the team examine whether differences in total yield reflected
        survival, production among survivors or both.
      </p>

      <p>
        The original question about production still mattered, and the deaths
        had raised a further question of their own.
        Recording how many plants died in each plot would let the team compare
        varieties on survival as well as yield.
        If some varieties lost fewer plants than others, the team could
        investigate why.
        If disease proved to be the cause, better survival could point to
        varieties that were less susceptible.
      </p>

      <p>
        Roger's advice is to always ask what the zeros represent and consider
        what might be learned from them.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-making-sense-of-differences-anova">
    <title>Analysis of Variance as a Descriptive Tool</title>

    <introduction>

      <p>
      <em>Source podcasts: 151 — Data Variability; 232 — ANOVA and Degrees of Freedom; 233 — Analysis of Variance as a Descriptive Tool</em>
      </p>

      <audio href="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/7181/151-data-variability.mp3" width="60%" />

      <audio href="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/9291/232-anova-and-degrees-of-freedom.mp3" width="60%" />

      <audio href="https://www.idems.international/podcast-player/9305/233-an-analysis-of-anova-as-a-descriptive-tool.mp3" width="60%" />

      <p>
      The separate summaries show how survival and production among survivors
      can both contribute to differences in total yield.
      When several variables may account for the same differences in the data, a
      model can help show what each adds.
      </p>

      <p>
      While supporting research in Burkina Faso, David encountered a student
      struggling to interpret an on-station experiment.
      The software he was using encouraged him to analyse one or two variables
      at a time.
      When more of the available information was considered together, the
      results became more intelligible.
      </p>

      <p>
      This illustrates an important use of analysis of variance, or ANOVA.
      Attention often goes straight to whether a result is statistically
      significant, but ANOVA also helps us explore what accounts for the
      variation we see.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-making-sense-of-differences-what-information-accounts-for">
      <title>What does the available information account for?</title>

      <p>
        A model is simply a description of the data.
        The simplest model gives every field the same average yield.
        Including variety in the model would give a separate average for each
        variety.
        If those averages differ, they give a closer description of the
        measured yields than one overall average.
      </p>

      <p>
        ANOVA measures how much this improves the description and how much
        variation remains.
        For example, adding fertiliser to a model that already includes variety
        shows how much more of the yield differences it accounts for.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-making-sense-of-differences-what-each-variable-adds">
      <title>What does each variable add?</title>

      <p>
        David uses a teaching game involving village, crop variety and
        fertiliser to show how recorded information can overlap.
        A model that includes village alone shows substantial differences in
        average yield between villages.
        Knowing which village a field belongs to tells us something about its
        yield.
      </p>

      <p>
        Adding variety and fertiliser after village accounts for some of the
        variation that remains.
        That raises a further question.
        How much does village add if variety and fertiliser go into the model
        first?
      </p>

      <p>
        With variety and fertiliser already in the model, adding village
        accounts for little further variation.
        The varieties farmers grew and the amounts of fertiliser they used
        already account for most of the difference between village averages.
      </p>

      <p>
        In a sequential ANOVA table, changing the order of the variables reveals
        where their contributions overlap.
        The full model, containing all three variables, gives the same predicted
        yield for each field and leaves the same variation unaccounted for in
        either order.
        What changes is how the table assigns the contribution they share.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-making-sense-of-differences-how-much-detail">
      <title>How much detail is useful?</title>

      <p>
        Choosing which variables to include still leaves a choice about how to
        represent them.
        With fertiliser, a straight line describes yield as changing by a steady
        amount for each additional unit applied.
        Estimating a separate average yield for each rate allows a more varied
        response.
        Yields might rise at low rates, then level off or fall at higher rates.
        That is an important consideration when deciding whether applying more
        fertiliser is likely to increase yield.
      </p>

      <p>
        The comparison uses both versions of fertiliser in the same model.
        The numerical version enters first to fit the straight-line trend,
        followed by fertiliser as a factor with a separate level for each rate.
        The sequential ANOVA shows how much additional variation the separate
        averages account for beyond the straight line.
      </p>

      <p>
        The amount of detail affects how many values the model has to estimate.
        A straight line needs two values, its height and slope.
        Giving each of the seven fertiliser rates its own average requires
        seven.
        The separate averages therefore use five more degrees of freedom,
        leaving fewer for estimating the spread around them.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a step by step activity using ANOVA to explore data, see the
        <em>Taming the Analysis of Variance</em> exercise.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-making-sense-of-differences-what-remains">
    <title>What Remains Unaccounted For?</title>

    <introduction>

      <p>
      <em>Source podcasts: <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/151-data-variability/">151: Data Variability</url>; <url href="https://www.idems.international/podcast/233-an-analysis-of-anova-as-a-descriptive-tool/">233: An Analysis of ANOVA as a Descriptive Tool</url>.
      Study: Buerkert, Stern and Marschner (1995), <url href="https://doi.org/10.2134/agronj1995.00021962008700040024x">Post Stratification Clarifies Treatment Effects on Pearl Millet Growth in the Sahel</url>.</em>
      </p>

      <p>
      Residual variation is often treated as what remains after the interesting
      work has been completed.
      Yet examining what the model leaves unaccounted for is part of judging the
      analysis.
      A residual is the difference between an observed value and the value
      fitted by the model.
      </p>

      <p>
      Some spread is expected even when observations are made under similar
      conditions.
      Measurement error, differences in growing conditions and relationships the
      model has missed can also contribute.
      Earlier studies that measured similar outcomes under comparable conditions
      can give a sense of how much spread to expect.
      </p>

      <p>
      Looking at the individual residuals shows whether the spread comes from a
      few large differences or many smaller ones.
      A few observations that lie far above or below their fitted values can
      account for as much residual variation as many smaller differences.
      These unusually large residuals can flag possible outliers.
      As Chapter 5 showed, returning to the original records to find out what
      happened can help researchers decide how to treat those observations in
      the analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
      If a group of observations tends to fall above or below the values fitted
      by the model, researchers can ask what those observations have in common.
      They may already have information that helps account for the pattern, or
      they may learn more by returning to the field or speaking with farmers.
      What they learn may change how they interpret the original comparison.
      The pattern may also point to a question that needs further evidence.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-making-sense-of-differences-mapping-residuals">
      <title>Mapping Residuals</title>

      <p>
        In a three-year pearl-millet trial at Sadoré, Niger, researchers tested
        several factors together, including crop residues, phosphorus and millet
        variety.
        They compared two rates of millet stalks spread on the soil surface, 500
        and 2,000 kilograms per hectare.
        Both rates were tested with and without added phosphorus.
      </p>

      <p>
        The field looked fairly uniform before the trial, apart from a few
        patches where the soil differed in colour.
        Once the millet began growing, plants in some parts of the field grew
        much better than others.
        How much of that difference came from the treatments, and how much
        reflected conditions in the field?
      </p>

      <p>
        After the first harvest, they compared each plot's grain yield with the
        average for its treatment combination.
        They marked each residual on a field map.
        The map revealed a pattern from west to east.
        Accounting for plot location reduced the remaining variation in grain
        yield, and the original treatment comparison held up.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pattern also raised a question for further investigation.
        Could a neighbouring fallow area to the west have sheltered a crop pest
        that contributed to the differences in yield?
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-making-sense-of-differences-where-treatments-helped">
      <title>Where Did the Treatments Help?</title>

      <p>
        They used the same approach to calculate residuals for early growth.
        Early in the first season, with the treatments already in place, they
        had scored how well the millet was growing.
        They compared each plot's score with the average for the two plots that
        had received the same treatment combination.
        This gave them an indication of how well each plot supported growth
        after allowing for the treatment it had received.
      </p>

      <p>
        They used each plot's early-growth residual when they analysed the grain
        and straw harvested from that plot.
        In the first year, this helped account for some of the differences in
        yield among plots given the same treatment.
        With less variation left unaccounted for, they could judge the treatment
        effects more precisely and address the trial's original question.
      </p>

      <p>
        The early-growth residuals also raised a new question.
        Did the treatments help to the same extent on more and less productive
        plots?
        They ranked the plots by their first-year early-growth residuals,
        divided the ranking in half and made the same treatment comparisons
        within each group.
        In the first two years, the higher rate of crop residues increased grain
        yield by an average of 48 per cent and straw yield by 42 per cent in the
        less productive plots.
        It made little difference in the more productive plots.
        Phosphorus helped in both groups.
        Why some plots were more productive remained a separate question.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-making-sense-of-differences-making-analysis-useful">
    <title>Making the Analysis Useful</title>

    <p>
      Making sense of variation means figuring out what the differences in the
      data represent and what they let us say about the question we're asking.
      Once you have a result, ask what else you know that might help you
      understand it better.
    </p>

    <p>
      A low millet yield might stand out when you look across all farms and look
      quite ordinary alongside yields from other members of the same farmer
      organisation.
      If two plots gave different harvests, knowing how many plants survived can
      help you see whether one plot had fewer plants at harvest or whether its
      surviving plants yielded less.
    </p>

    <p>
      At Sadoré, taking plot location into account helped account for some of
      the spread in grain yields, and the treatment comparison held up.
      The early-growth scores let the team ask whether the treatment worked as
      well on more productive plots as on less productive ones.
      That comparison showed where the extra crop residues helped most.
    </p>

    <p>
      Go back to the question you started with and ask whether you would now
      give the same answer.
      If the treatment helped mainly under particular conditions, say which
      ones.
      If you can see where a difference occurs and its cause is still uncertain,
      describe what you found.
      That gives the next study a more precise question to ask.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-making-sense-of-differences-reference">
    <title>Reference</title>

    <p>
      Buerkert, A., Stern, R. D. and Marschner, H. (1995).
      Post stratification clarifies treatment effects on pearl millet growth in
      the Sahel.
      <em>Agronomy Journal</em>, 87(4), 752–761.
      <url href="https://doi.org/10.2134/agronj1995.00021962008700040024x">https://doi.org/10.2134/agronj1995.00021962008700040024x</url>
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
